WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:03.170
From the U.S.-China trade war,

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:06.250
a controversial security law in Hong Kong,

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:08.370
building in the South China Sea,

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:11.130
to China's global infrastructure projects,

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.460
there is undeniably a Cold War-like battle

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.860
for influence on all fronts.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.403
And that includes space.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.740
We're in a space race today,

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:22.663
just as we were in the 1960s

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:25.280
and the stakes are even higher.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:28.560
Although China hasn't come close

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.180
to achieving what the U.S.
has, they're catching up.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.330
Since 2018, China has launched
more rockets into orbit

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:36.603
than any other country.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.930
In 2019, they became the
first to land a rover

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:42.330
on the far side of the moon.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:46.330
And in 2020, China
released the last satellite

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:48.840
in their global navigation
satellite system,

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.573
called Beidou, China's
answer to the U.S.-made GPS.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.170
Like China's big infrastructure
projects here on Earth,

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:59.720
their goals are to get a
foothold in space technology

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.230
that will drive profit,

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.030
make them independent
from U.S. technology,

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.640
get an upper hand in military operations

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.033
and show superiority on the world stage.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.910
With Joe Biden in the White House,

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:15.590
experts think space spending could slow,

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:17.610
although the Democratic Party has stated

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:18.960
that it will continue the goal

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.373
of sending Americans back to
the moon and beyond to Mars.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:25.690
We are launching humans to the moon

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:28.503
for the first time since 1972.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.440
And that might not be a bad thing.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:33.830
While a Cold War scenario

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:37.130
has dangerous political
and economic implications,

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:39.770
a race to the cosmos,
like any good rivalry,

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:42.973
could be a huge benefit for
space and other industries.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:47.020
If you think that the
U.S.-China competition

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:49.620
is going to be not just
a clash of strategies,

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:50.860
but a clash of systems,

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.060
it's going to be a contest over whether

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:54.970
American-style liberal democracy

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:57.660
or Chinese-style authoritarian capitalism

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.270
is best suited to the
demands of the 21st century,

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:02.080
then it's entirely possible

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.370
that you could see the United States

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:07.180
make investments in
infrastructure and education

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:08.480
and technological innovation

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.030
and basic research

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:11.940
that it wouldn't otherwise make

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.733
as a result of that competition.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:24.220
In 1957, the Soviet
Union shocked the world

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:27.983
by putting the first ever
satellite into space, Sputnik 1.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:32.960
There is real military
significance to these launchings.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:36.010
The satellite's unanticipated success

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:38.450
brought on the American Sputnik crisis,

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:40.730
a period of public fear and anxiety

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:42.810
in Western nations about the perceived

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.180
technological gap
between the United States

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:46.183
and Soviet Union.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:51.840
And so the National Defense
Education Act of 1957

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.590
is passed in response to Sputnik.

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.880
And it includes major investments

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:58.760
in a variety of intellectual pursuits

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.670
that were considered
critical to staying ahead

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:01.760
in the Cold War.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:05.980
And so the rise of entire
industries like aerospace,

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:09.270
the rise of entire regions
like Silicon Valley

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:12.410
or Orange County,
semiconductors, the Internet,

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:14.760
a variety of other
things that we associate

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.570
with the rise of the information age,

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:18.080
were originally conceived

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.510
as part of the military industrial complex

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:22.830
in the 1950s and 1960s.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.730
And let me say that our
scientists and engineers,

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:28.190
in offering their services to
the government in this field,

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:31.273
have been generous, patriotic and prompt.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:34.820
And nowhere was government spending

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:36.750
more apparent and highly publicized

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:38.453
as the space race of the time.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:42.130
In the Eisenhower years
and then Kennedy years,

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:45.550
the U.S. started pouring
money into the space program.

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:48.943
By, I think mid-1960s, the
U.S. was spending about

00:03:48.943 --> 00:03:51.563
4% of its total budget on space.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:55.010
It was the largest
percentage of the budget

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:56.993
spent on space in American history.

00:03:58.150 --> 00:03:59.750
And the intense rivalry allowed

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:01.410
for some of the biggest accomplishments

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.630
humanity has ever seen.

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:07.270
Including the first living
animal to enter space by Russia.

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:10.280
The first man to enter space by Russia.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:13.163
And the first probe to land
on the moon, also Russia.

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:18.220
And so that led to a huge
surge in U.S. spending

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:19.430
on the space program,

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:22.300
which culminated in
1969 with Neil Armstrong

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:24.563
and Buzz Aldrin stepping on the moon.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:35.790
The moon landing is considered

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:38.000
a conclusion to the space race.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.586
And as U.S.-Soviet relations
improved, space spending waned.

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:47.430
I think that one of the
reasons that the U.S.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:48.953
has not been as focused on space

00:04:48.953 --> 00:04:51.063
is because the end of the Cold War.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:54.010
Since the Apollo program,

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:57.160
the NASA budget has
hovered around 0.5% to 1%.

00:04:58.010 --> 00:04:59.530
But in the last few decades,

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:03.300
the world has seen the
rise of a new power, China.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.360
While governed by an authoritarian regime

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:07.070
like the USSR was,

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:09.520
China also has far more economic strength

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:13.260
and greater resources than
the Soviet Union ever did.

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:14.713
And that worries the U.S.

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:18.010
The president has already signed into law

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:20.430
the largest NASA budget

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:24.160
since the days of the Apollo program.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:26.830
There is very much an
ideological challenge.

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:30.100
It's sort of a contest
over which sort of system,

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:33.430
democracy or authoritarian capitalism,

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:35.980
will win the day in the 21st century.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:38.140
And I think what American officials

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:41.860
fear is that the Chinese
will not be content

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:44.790
to have second-tier status in a world

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:47.503
that is led by the United
States and its allies.

00:05:54.646 --> 00:05:56.180
But space isn't simply a show

00:05:56.180 --> 00:06:00.357
of technological strength like
it was during the Cold War.

00:06:00.357 --> 00:06:02.250
The space industry is now worth

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:05.710
an estimated $345 billion.

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:07.610
With technology that we use every day

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:10.800
depending on it, like GPS.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.730
China started launching an alternative

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:16.070
to the American system,
called Beidou in 2000.

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:17.650
The recently completed version

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:20.220
is 20 centimeters more accurate than GPS

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:21.557
by some estimates.

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:25.810
China's Beidou space program

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:29.760
brings prestige and practical benefits,

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:32.750
the prestige from announcing their arrival

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:36.790
as a power in space similar
to the prestige which the US

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:39.770
achieved from its first moon landing.

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:44.340
The practical benefits, both
military and commercial.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:48.530
If you wanna target a missile
or a fast-food delivery,

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:50.940
you need a GPS system.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:52.640
With its Beidou system,

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:56.800
China has its own GPS capability in place.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:00.000
And so independence from U.S. influence

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.480
on that key part of infrastructure

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:03.903
in the information economy.

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:07.190
China even opened up

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:09.650
the private space industry in 2014

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:11.470
in the hope of encouraging competitors

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:13.770
to the U.S. private space industry.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:16.250
iSpace was the first
private company in China

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:18.530
to successfully launch
a rocket and satellite

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:20.640
into orbit in 2019.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:22.570
And a company called Galaxy Space

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:26.130
plans to launch 650
low-Earth orbit satellites

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:28.380
similar to SpaceX's Starlink,

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:30.930
which will give better,
faster internet access

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:32.433
to people all over the world.

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:37.420
But it's not all about business.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.440
I think that for President Xi Jinping,

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:49.840
this Chinese space program
is hugely important,

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:51.450
not just for all the
practical applications

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:53.070
but also just for prestige.

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:56.160
It's important for
President Xi to show that

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:57.400
China is a superpower.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:07:59.023
Superpowers have space programs.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:03.850
China will have to prove

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:05.610
that they can perform the kind of missions

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:08.600
NASA has done and
possibly go even further.

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:12.070
Like China's recent moon rover, Chang'e-4.

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:13.500
Although it was the third country

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:15.010
to land a rover on the moon,

00:08:15.010 --> 00:08:18.140
it was the first to land on
the far side of the moon.

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:21.170
And China's recently launched
Mars rover isn't the first,

00:08:21.170 --> 00:08:24.263
but it's the first orbiter
lander rover all in one mission.

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:29.360
China even has a Voyager-like mission

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:31.400
in its initial planning stages

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:33.260
that will do a flyby of Neptune

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:35.540
and then out to explore
interstellar space.

00:08:35.540 --> 00:08:37.420
But more importantly, China will build

00:08:37.420 --> 00:08:39.100
its own space station,

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:40.960
which will serve as an essential tool

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:43.676
for tests and research in space

00:08:43.676 --> 00:08:47.093
and offer an alternative to the
International Space Station.

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:51.060
When it comes to innovation,

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:53.120
China will have some advantages

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:57.310
when it comes to directing
the entire resources

00:08:57.310 --> 00:09:00.500
of a state or the entire
resources of a society

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:03.940
toward some particular
technological challenge,

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:05.600
AI, for instance.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:09.150
But the cost of that is
there is less of an open

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:12.350
economic and intellectual
ecosystem in China

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:14.410
than there is in the United States.

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:17.070
There's more inherent
dynamism in a society

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:20.600
where you have truly
open information flows

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:22.500
and where innovation is as much

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:25.600
a bottom-up, as it is
a top-down phenomenon.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:27.150
And so that's not to say
that the United States

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:29.870
is destined to win the race for AI or 5G

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:31.140
or any other technology.

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:33.630
I think there's real danger
in a lot of these fields.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:35.870
But if the United States
puts forth the energy

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:38.520
and puts forth the
investment that's necessary

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:40.500
in these areas, there's no inherent reason

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:43.343
that it can't succeed
because it is a democracy.

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:46.830
And there are signs

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:49.230
that China's space program is stumbling.

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:53.130
In 2020, a long march 5B
rocket scattered debris

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:55.400
over Cote d'Ivoire, after an uncontrolled

00:09:55.400 --> 00:09:57.563
re-entry of the rocket's core stage.

00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:00.850
This was the largest botch
of its kind in years.

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:06.060
So the worry is that China
is just not that transparent

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:07.300
when it comes to this sort of thing.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:08.710
So, was this just a one-off?

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:10.910
Was this just something went wrong,

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:12.720
or is this just an example

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:14.770
of what might be happening going forward?

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:21.920
The competition may prove to be too much.

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:24.280
China is up against not
only a space program

00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:26.560
with a long and decorated history.

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:29.800
But one with more ambitious
missions than ever before.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:32.570
The U.S. is now trying to
beat China back to the moon

00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:35.030
to build a lunar base, harvest resources

00:10:35.030 --> 00:10:37.610
and then use it as a stepping
stone to get to Mars.

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:39.140
This time when we go to the moon,

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:41.490
we're gonna go to stay with a purpose

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:43.540
of learning how to live
and work on another world

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:46.563
so we can take that knowledge
and information to Mars.

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:50.930
I think that the sense of urgency

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:52.590
that we're hearing from
the Trump administration

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:55.010
about the need to accelerate
the Artemis program

00:10:55.010 --> 00:10:57.250
to get to the moon by 2024,

00:10:57.250 --> 00:10:58.150
I think part of that

00:10:58.150 --> 00:11:01.690
is because of the perceived
threat from China.

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:06.610
So in that sense, I think
that having China as

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:09.620
a more serious player in space

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:11.830
could potentially be good for NASA,

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:13.630
good for the American space program.

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:17.620
Other NASA projects include a Mars mission

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:19.090
equipped with a mini helicopter

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:21.740
to fly on the surface of the red planet,

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:24.240
another mini helicopter
mission planned for Titan,

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:26.090
Saturn's largest moon,

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:28.810
and the launch of the
James Webb Space Telescope,

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:31.040
an upgrade to the Hubble Space Telescope

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:32.960
whose photographs
changed our understanding

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:34.460
of the universe as we know it.

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:39.510
The ambitions of China and the U.S.

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:42.580
may depend on how long
their rivalry continues.

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:45.440
If history is any lesson,
the bigger the rivalry,

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:47.470
the further the two space superpowers

00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:49.053
are likely to push themselves.

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:53.980
The overall goal with
China's space program,

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:57.560
I think, is to match the United States.

00:11:57.560 --> 00:12:00.730
China does not want to be
subordinate to the U.S. on Earth

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:02.860
and doesn't want to be
subordinate to the U.S. in space.

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:06.750
Right now, the plan is
to try to narrow the gap.

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:08.160
And so if you are looking at this

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:10.730
from an American strategic perspective,

00:12:10.730 --> 00:12:14.600
the concern would be that
China may have a jump

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:16.760
in the areas of high-tech innovation

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:17.950
that will be most important

00:12:17.950 --> 00:12:20.950
in terms of powering
innovation and productivity

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:22.010
during the 21st century.

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:24.980
And that can perhaps be
turned into advantages

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:26.020
on the battlefield.

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:29.130
I should say that the
jury is really still out

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:31.730
in terms of how effective China will be

00:12:31.730 --> 00:12:34.290
at mastering innovation in these areas.

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:36.570
But if China continues to lack

00:12:36.570 --> 00:12:38.700
the open informational ecosystem

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:40.820
that we've typically associated

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:42.500
with success and innovation,

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:45.050
there may still be significant obstacles

00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:47.610
to maintaining the dynamism needed

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:49.452
to spur innovation over the long run.

